export USAGE_REPORTING:
    coverage:
        Provide comprehensive usage totals and trends by agent, harness,
        task, and model where the available telemetry supports attribution.
        Include shared account quotas, reset times, remaining capacity,
        projected exhaustion, and costs when available. Preserve unknown
        values and label estimates under the existing estimate policy.
    history:
        Usage history covers the whole orchestration, not only the live run.
        Rebuild it from the records each harness already keeps locally, such
        as session transcripts, session databases or session export
        commands. Count each billed message once, deduplicated by its
        message identity, at its own timestamp and model. A live source that
        repeats the same messages must not add to totals a second time.
        Quota percentages that a provider only reports live, and that were
        not sampled earlier, stay unknown and are never backfilled.
    projections:
        For every quota, show the time it is projected to be reached,
        compared with its reset time, as an estimate with its basis. Quota
        percentages project from a trend over the samples of the current
        accounting period, and stay unknown with too few samples or a flat
        or falling trend. A plan quota that a provider does not expose can
        come from a snapshot the user supplies (total, used, time read).
        Calibrate it against the token history up to that time to estimate
        units per token, then project current use and exhaustion from the
        trailing burn. The calibration ratio is shown and labeled as an
        estimate.
    ratePerHour:
        Show the percentage of each quota consumed per hour, as a chart and
        as a current rate beside each quota. Hours without samples are gaps,
        never zero.
    costs:
        When providers publish pay-as-you-go token rates, estimate costs
        from them, and label the result as an estimate at API rates, since
        actual spend depends on the subscription or plan. Prefer
        machine-readable price lists, fetched at most daily and cached, and
        show the age of the rates. When a provider publishes rates only on a
        web page, pin them in a local override that records the source and
        the date read. Bill reasoning tokens at the output rate unless the
        provider says otherwise. Tokens without a rate have unknown cost,
        are counted as unpriced, and are never shown as zero.
    perMinuteLimits:
        When a provider enforces per-minute request or token limits, or a
        concurrency limit, and the orchestrator throttles agents to stay
        under them, show each model's observed rate against its limit and
        the orchestrator's current admission cap. Label the rates as
        estimates averaged over their window, and explain in one line what
        the limits are and what the cap does.
    accounting:
        Distinguish cumulative totals from usage increments. Do not add a
        cumulative total as though it were a new increment. Associate
        measurements with their reporting source and accounting period so
        overlapping observations do not count the same usage twice.
        Identify shared quotas and count each quota once across the agents
        that use it; do not multiply shared capacity by the number of agents.
    presentation:
        Show units, accounting periods, and attribution coverage with usage
        and trends. Distinguish unavailable costs from zero cost. Do not
        combine costs in different currencies as one total without an
        explicitly defined conversion. Distinguish known usage from estimates.
    acceptanceCriteria:
        - Available usage can be examined by agent, harness, task, and model.
        - Cumulative totals and increments do not double count the same consumption.
        - Multiple agents sharing an account expose one shared quota.
        - Available reset times, remaining capacity, exhaustion projections, and costs appear.
        - Unsupported breakdowns, quotas, projections, or costs are explicitly unknown.
        - Usage history reaches back to the start of the orchestration, with every billed message counted once.
        - Each quota shows its projected limit-reached time and its percentage consumed per hour.
        - Cost estimates name their rate source and its date, and unpriced tokens are never counted as zero.

export USAGE_OPEN_QUESTIONS:
    - Which reporting periods and history retention are needed for usage trends?
    - How are shared account identities and overlapping usage observations correlated?
